  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They never led and still found a way to stay in it.",
    "what_this_means": "Baltimore scored first and never looked back, but the Athletics clawed their way to 3 runs in 11 RISP situations. Perkins threw five solid innings, giving up just one early, and the bullpen followed with four straight scoreless. Suarez, Medina, Roycroft, and Alvarado combining for four IP with four strikedowns and zero damage. The problem wasn't the pitching. It was that the offense converted runners into runs just three times all night, leaving seven on base across nine innings. Cortes drove in a run, McNeil had two hits, but 10 total hits never translated into pressure. By the seventh inning, with three runners in scoring position and the game still close, nothing came. That's where this one ended.",
    "what_to_watch": "Tight games with limited offense have become this team's signature. And they can't keep winning them. Jack Perkins delivered a line good enough to win, and the bullpen protected it through the late innings. But converting three of eleven RISP situations is not sustainable, especially when you're chasing. The seventh inning told the story: runners on base, ball in play, nothing to show for it. This is the gap between staying close and actually winning close games.",
